    "translation": "Among younger generations tired of city life, moving to rural areas is attracting attention. A major factor is that with the spread of telework, living in the countryside while remaining employed at a company in the city has become a realistic option. The number of people visiting municipal consultation desks grows every year, and the core of them, it is said, is no longer people of post-retirement age as before, but working people in their thirties and forties.\nWhat those who have relocated unanimously point to are lower living costs and abundant nature. Rent is less than half that of the city center, and crowded commutes are a thing of the past. Many families decided to relocate because it is easier to raise children. Meanwhile, for the regions that receive them, migrants are looked to as people who will soften population decline and bring new ideas to the community.\nHowever, relocating on longing alone can easily end in failure. In rural areas a car is indispensable, and in some places medical institutions and shopping options are limited. There are in fact people who cannot fit into local relationships and return to the city. That is precisely why many municipalities recommend a \"trial relocation\"—living in the area for a short period before making the decision. A decision made with knowledge of the realities of daily life is the very key to making a relocation last.",
