    "translation": "\"Food loss\"—still-edible food being thrown away in large quantities—has become a problem. Food loss generated in Japan in a single year amounts to roughly five million tons, and nearly half of it is said to come from households. The accumulation of small everyday actions—buying too much and letting food pass its date, cooking too much and leaving leftovers—produces enormous waste.\nBusinesses also bear part of the cause. At retail stores, the custom of keeping shelves always fully stocked remains deep-rooted, and unsold items tend to be discarded. It has also been pointed out that a business custom—dividing the period from the date of manufacture to the best-before date into three equal parts and not delivering to stores any product past the first third—has led to the disposal of food that could still be eaten.\nMoves to change this situation have begun. Stores that discount and sell out products nearing their dates are appearing, and donations to organizations that deliver surplus food to people in need are also spreading. Thanks to technological progress, some companies have introduced systems that forecast sales and adjust purchase volumes. Reducing food loss not only lessens the burden on the environment but also saves money for households and companies. Each person turning the feeling of \"mottainai\"—what a waste—into action will be the first step.",
