    "translation": "In the shopping streets in front of train stations, stores with their shutters permanently lowered refuse to disappear. With customers lost to large suburban stores and online shopping, compounded by aging owners and a shortage of successors, many of the nation's shopping streets face decline. According to one survey, the proportion of vacant stores in shopping streets has doubled over the past twenty years.\nSome shopping streets, however, have defied this trend and regained their bustle. In one regional city, the shopping street lowered the rent on vacant stores and leased them to young entrepreneurs, drawing in distinctive businesses such as cafes and craft workshops. Rather than competing with big stores on the same merchandise, they competed on value found nowhere else—things you can buy only there, people you can meet only there. Morning markets and festivals held jointly by the shop owners also gave local people a reason to visit.\nWhat this example shows is a change in the role of the shopping street. Merely as a place to sell goods, shopping streets are probably no match for big stores or online retail. But their value as places where people meet face to face and community ties are nurtured is, if anything, rising. Reviving a shopping street may mean not restoring its former appearance, but finding a new role.",
