    "translation": "As working from home has spread, voices saying \"employees should come to the office every day after all\" have been growing louder again. The main reasons given are that good ideas are not born without face-to-face contact and that young employees cannot be properly trained. It is true that new projects sometimes emerge from casual chat, and it is also a fact that the finer tricks of a job are hard to convey through a screen. Such criticisms carry a certain persuasive force.\nHowever, that does not mean everyone needs to come to the office every day. It means a great deal that employees who used to spend two hours a day commuting can now use that time for family or study. For people dealing with childcare or nursing care, whether remote work is available can even determine whether they can keep working at all. Indeed, at my own workplace too, I have heard again and again from people who say that thanks to remote work they did not have to resign. Moreover, if the system were abruptly abolished, talented people could well drift away to other companies.\nMy view is that the most realistic approach is to set one or two days a week when people meet face to face, while otherwise letting each person choose where to work. The real question is not the binary choice between office and home, but whether we can combine ways of working according to the purpose of the work.",
